\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\title{Motivation and aim}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The core interest is the neuron, its synapses and the whole brain as
dynamical processes with nonlinear behavior: bifurcations, attractors
and chaos. A few nonlinear differential equations can look innocuous,
hinting at nothing exotic, yet they show how a system pushed past a
critical point snaps into a qualitatively different regime, with
outcomes highly sensitive near that point; in neurons, rest gives way to
tonic spiking, bursting or irregular firing.
\citet{§1.2, pp. 8–10}{izhikevich2007dynamical} treats the neuron as a
dynamical system and shows rest, periodic spiking and bursting, and
\citet{§3.1, p. 49}{ermentrout2010mathematical} reads the solutions of
such equations as curves in phase space. This richness develops from
single neurons through synapses and networks to the whole brain.}

\p{A naive model that treats external stimuli as the primary driver of
neural activity gives a static map from stimulus to response. It does
not capture that the same stimulus can produce drastically different
responses depending on the system's prior trajectory. For one model
neuron, \citet{§3.3.2, p. 53}{ermentrout2010mathematical} records that a
small perturbation of voltage from rest decays back to rest, while a
sufficiently large perturbation makes an action potential, and
\citet{Figure 3.1a, pp. 50–51}{ermentrout2010mathematical} draws the two
outcomes. \citet{§1.1.2, p. 3}{izhikevich2007dynamical} finds no single
voltage that separates the trials which spike from the trials which do
not. This points to an ongoing activity that constitutes a background
dynamical process under such influence. Think of an ocean: it is a
natural flow that carries the system on its own. When a stimulus enters
the flow, like a drop into the ocean, what we observe is only its
surface expression. The books cited here treat single model neurons, and
for networks this reading is our view.}

\p{We therefore take the state and its evolution as the object of study,
and a recorded voltage as one coordinate of that state. Which evolution
of the state produces the voltage that a recording keeps?}

\p{Control theory enters in two ways. The brain is itself a control
system, with its own control behaviors such as motor control and
homeostasis. The brain is also an object that can be observed, steered
or triggered from outside, through stimulation and brain–computer
interfaces.}

\p{Modeling the brain in a control-theoretic way as one object would
blur distinctions that matter. We therefore factorize it into layers:
measurement, state, behavior, and intervention, where measurement is
what we record of the brain, since we never access its state directly,
and intervention is what acts on the brain's dynamics, including sensory
input and deliberate stimulation from outside.}

\p{Structure and development complete the picture: how a neuron is
built, how a general cell develops into a neuron, how synapses grow and
are refined from infancy to adulthood, and how that growth produces
external output in behavior.}

\p{FNIP studies mathematics motivated by these questions of neuron
interaction, in the way FDIP studies mathematics motivated by physics,
in the same family as FTIP and the other math notes in utensil/forest.
The biology serves as motivating worked examples only; no biological
theory is proposed. The methods familiar to neuroscientists are treated
as application-level tools, and each is recovered from a deeper
mathematical statement: a limit, a reduction or a special case. Each
chapter keeps a remark relating that derivation to the familiar method,
and a worked example that carries out the recovery on a standard neuron
model.}

\p{We study the nonlinear dynamics of neurons, synapses and the brain
through dynamical systems, and the higher function and emergent behavior
they give rise to. We also study this behavior through the lens of
control theory, looking at how the brain acts as a controller and how it
may be steered by stimulation and neural interfaces. We then take a
deeper dive into how a neuron is built and develops from a general cell
into circuits capable of self-sustained activity, both reactive and
proactive.}
